  "analysis": "Comparing candidate platforms against selection criteria reveals a core architectural tradeoff between orchestration depth and routing specialization. DeepAgents (source_001) inherits LangGraph's graph state machine capabilities (source_002), giving it durable state, check-pointing, and explicit node/edge control—strengths for complex multi-step workflows where recovery and observability matter. However, this generality means routing is one concern among many; per-node model selection must be layered on top rather than being the framework's native focus. DSH (source_004) is explicitly scoped to the outer validation environment, not the core routing loop, so it cannot serve as the primary routing engine; its value is as a secondary guardrail or evaluation harness, which adds operational surface area but isolates validation from production routing. The benchmark design (source_007) demands fixed task DAGs, per-node and per-task scoring, cost accounting, and critical-path latency—criteria that favor a platform with first-class DAG execution and instrumentation. LangGraph-based DeepAgents satisfies the DAG execution requirement directly, but cost and latency accounting must be implemented as custom instrumentation. The research gap (source_005) frames the selection problem as optimizing a quality-cost-latency Pareto frontier per node, which implies the platform must support heterogeneous model assignment at node granularity without forcing a single global model. DeepAgents can do this via graph configuration, but the routing decision logic remains external. The decomposition boundary (source_006) clarifies that static task decomposition is an application-layer concern; the router receives the DAG as input. This reduces the platform requirement to faithful DAG execution plus per-node model invocation, which DeepAgents meets and DSH does not (by design). Tradeoff summary: DeepAgents offers orchestration maturity and state durability at the cost of routing-specific overhead and custom benchmarking instrumentation; DSH offers clean separation of validation but no core routing capability; a combined architecture uses DeepAgents for the routing loop and DSH for outer validation, trading integration complexity for clearer failure isolation. The scoring rubric (source_008) rewards evidence accuracy and analysis depth, so the comparison must weight architectural fit over feature count.",
